<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the desk remembers and judges (15-18); half-time on the enterprise floor. Now it learns who decides.*

### Half-time

Four lessons ago the desk gained a memory: every act is an event (15), counted (16), judged (17) and provable after the fact (18). That is already more enterprise substrate than most systems ever grow — and if you stopped any night this week, that is the work working, not you failing.

Now the floor's second half: judgment. Who approves a refund is a question whose answer changes quarterly, and the desk is about to hold it as data.

<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

Who should approve a refund? Under 100, nobody; up to 500, the team lead; above that, finance; gold customers one level lower. That table changes every quarter and the business wants to change it without a deploy. It is a **decision**, and in this stack decisions are DMN.

> **A decision decides. It never acts, never validates, and never authorizes.**

The decision answers "who approves?". The action (lesson 20) acts on the answer. A rule that guards a mutation belongs in the action; put it in a decision and every caller that does not ask the decision bypasses it.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> The approval table changed every quarter in 2013, and in 2019, and it will again. Now it changes by publishing, not by deploying.</p>
</div>

## Decisions are resources

`ash_decisions` provides two resource macros. The host already has both:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition do
  use AshDecisions.Resources.Definition,
    domain: AshEnterprise.Decisions,
    repo: AshEnterprise.Repo,
    table: "dmn_definitions",
    base: AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource,
    base_opts: [ownership: :organization_owned, lifecycle?: false, audit?: true, archival?: false]
end
```

* `Definition`: the DMN document, versioned, `draft → published → retired`. Publishing is one-way and audited.
* `Evaluation`: append-only evidence of what each invoked decision saw and decided.

Note `base: AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource`: the package's resources inherit your platform (tenancy, audit, policies) instead of bringing their own.

## The rules of the artifact

1. **The DMN XML is the single artifact.** Author it in the embedded dmn-js editor at `/app/decisions` (each definition's editor lives at `/app/decisions/<key>/editor`), or hand-write the XML — the fallback cell below is the proven headless path. There is no rule table in Postgres and no code DSL to keep in sync.
2. **Published definitions are immutable.** A change is a new version.
3. **Callers float to the latest published version by default**, and may pin. That is the opposite of processes (lesson 21), deliberately: a process version is a *shape* in-flight tokens stand on; a decision is a *rule* the business wants to take effect now.
4. **FEEL numbers are decimal.** Every context value goes through `AshDecisions.Feel.to_feel_value/2`. A raw Elixir integer makes `< 1000` a type error, which is `null`, which a table reads as "no rule matched": silently empty.
5. **`OUTPUT ORDER` and `RULE ORDER` hit policies are refused**, since they make rule sequence meaningful. Use `UNIQUE`, `ANY`, `FIRST`, `PRIORITY` or `COLLECT`.

## Author and publish

In the editor, create decision `refund_approver` with inputs `amount` (number) and `tier` (string), output `approver` (string), hit policy `FIRST`:

| amount | tier | approver |
|---|---|---|
| `< 100` | `-` | `"none"` |
| `<= 500` | `"gold"` | `"none"` |
| `<= 500` | `-` | `"team_lead"` |
| `-` | `"gold"` | `"team_lead"` |
| `-` | `-` | `"finance"` |

Publish it. A construct the compiler does not execute is refused **by element id** at publish time, not ignored.

> **No browser? Unlike processes (lesson 21), there is no mix-task path for *your tenant* here** — the deploy's `mix ash_enterprise.bpmn.publish` publishes `priv/dmn/*.dmn` baselines into the platform organization under its filename→key rule (`refund_approver.dmn` would land as key `refund.approver`, which lesson 21's ref and the query below will not match). The dmn-js editor at `/app/decisions` is the sanctioned UI; the cell below is the sanctioned headless fallback, driving the same resource the editor drives.

If you have no browser, author the XML by hand and publish through the resource — validate the inputs first, exactly as lesson 14 teaches:

```elixir
xml = """
<definitions xmlns="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20230324/MODEL/"
             id="refund_approver_definitions"
             name="Refund approver"
             namespace="https://ash-enterprise.example/dmn/refund-approver"
             expressionLanguage="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20230324/FEEL/"
             typeLanguage="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20230324/FEEL/">
  <inputData id="input_amount" name="amount">
    <variable id="var_amount" name="amount" typeRef="number"/>
  </inputData>
  <inputData id="input_tier" name="tier">
    <variable id="var_tier" name="tier" typeRef="string"/>
  </inputData>
  <decision id="decision_refund_approver" name="Refund approver">
    <variable id="var_approver" name="approver" typeRef="string"/>
    <informationRequirement id="req_amount"><requiredInput href="#input_amount"/></informationRequirement>
    <informationRequirement id="req_tier"><requiredInput href="#input_tier"/></informationRequirement>
    <decisionTable id="table_refund_approver" hitPolicy="FIRST" outputLabel="approver">
      <input id="clause_amount" label="Amount">
        <inputExpression id="expr_amount" typeRef="number"><text>amount</text></inputExpression>
      </input>
      <input id="clause_tier" label="Tier">
        <inputExpression id="expr_tier" typeRef="string"><text>tier</text></inputExpression>
      </input>
      <output id="clause_approver" name="approver" typeRef="string"/>
      <rule id="rule_small"><inputEntry id="e_small_amt"><text>&lt; 100</text></inputEntry><inputEntry id="e_small_tier"><text>-</text></inputEntry><outputEntry id="o_small"><text>"none"</text></outputEntry></rule>
      <rule id="rule_gold_mid"><inputEntry id="e_gold_mid_amt"><text>&lt;= 500</text></inputEntry><inputEntry id="e_gold_mid_tier"><text>"gold"</text></inputEntry><outputEntry id="o_gold_mid"><text>"none"</text></outputEntry></rule>
      <rule id="rule_mid"><inputEntry id="e_mid_amt"><text>&lt;= 500</text></inputEntry><inputEntry id="e_mid_tier"><text>-</text></inputEntry><outputEntry id="o_mid"><text>"team_lead"</text></outputEntry></rule>
      <rule id="rule_gold_high"><inputEntry id="e_gold_high_amt"><text>-</text></inputEntry><inputEntry id="e_gold_high_tier"><text>"gold"</text></inputEntry><outputEntry id="o_gold_high"><text>"team_lead"</text></outputEntry></rule>
      <rule id="rule_finance"><inputEntry id="e_fin_amt"><text>-</text></inputEntry><inputEntry id="e_fin_tier"><text>-</text></inputEntry><outputEntry id="o_fin"><text>"finance"</text></outputEntry></rule>
    </decisionTable>
  </decision>
</definitions>
"""

# The contract, from the tool: create takes {key, name, xml}.
AshAgentTools.validate_input(AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition, :create, %{
  "key" => "refund_approver",
  "name" => "Refund approver",
  "xml" => xml
}).valid?

# Find what you (or the editor) already published, else create the draft and publish.
definition =
  AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition
  |> Ash.Query.filter(key == "refund_approver" and status == :published)
  |> Ash.Query.sort(version: :desc)
  |> Ash.read_first!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)

definition ||
  AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition
  |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:create, %{key: "refund_approver", name: "Refund approver", xml: xml})
  |> Ash.create!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  |> AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition.publish!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
```

Note `create` is **not** the primary action, so `Ash.create!/2` with no `action:` refuses — go through `for_create(:create, ...)`, exactly as written. The draft carries the compiler's findings in `verification`; publish compiles again and refuses real errors.

## Evaluate

Evaluate the published definition for `amount: 250, tier: "standard"`, as the actor and tenant from lesson 15's fabric cell.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  require Ash.Query

  definition =
    AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition
    |> Ash.Query.filter(key == "refund_approver" and status == :published)
    |> Ash.Query.sort(version: :desc)
    |> Ash.read_first!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)

  AshDecisions.Evaluator.evaluate(definition, %{"amount" => Decimal.new(250), "tier" => "standard"},
    actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  ```

  For a dry run that records no `Evaluation` row, use `mix ash_agent.evaluate refund_approver '{"amount": 250, "tier": "standard"}'` (lesson 14). `actor` and `tenant` come from lesson 15's fabric cell. Field names on `Definition` vary by version; `AshAgentTools.describe_resource(AshEnterprise.Decisions.Definition)` is the authority. Two shapes to expect in the result: `outputs` is the **plain output value** under the FIRST hit policy (`"team_lead"`, not a map keyed by output name), and `matched_rule_ids` **over-reports** — it lists the matched rule *and* the catch-all that also matched, though only the first rule's output is used. Audit the outcome, not the id list.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** The published table answered `team_lead` — and the dry run recorded nothing, so tuning the rule is still free.

## The host seam

Processes (21) do not call the evaluator. They call the host's `AshEnterprise.Process.DecisionResolver`, which implements `decide/3` and `exists?/1`. `exists?/1` lets a BPMN diagram be verified at publish time against decisions that exist. Read it now — and note the tooling boundary: `AshAgentTools.resolve/1` addresses **Ash resources only**, and this is a plain behaviour module, so resolve raises on it. Read the file:

```elixir
path = Path.join(File.cwd!(), "lib/ash_enterprise/process/decision_resolver.ex")

path |> File.read!() |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.take(40) |> Enum.join("\n")
```

## Two things the engine will not tell you

* **Which rule fired is not recorded.** Do not compute a second opinion in Elixir: an answer that disagrees with the engine that actually decided is worse than none.
* **Every expression is hostile input.** Tenant admins write these. Size and depth are bounded, evaluation times out, external functions are refused. There is no escape hatch to call host code.

